WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.281
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book two.

2
00:00:05.581 --> 00:00:08.967
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.267 --> 00:00:17.039
Unit nine: Translation Theory. Today you will discuss possibility and obligation in translation with modal passives.

4
00:00:17.339 --> 00:00:20.061
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:24.082
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.917 --> 00:00:25.883
Fidelity.

7
00:00:26.883 --> 00:00:29.939
Fidelity means faithfulness to the original text.

8
00:00:30.550 --> 00:00:34.094
Perfect fidelity can make a translation sound unnatural.

9
00:00:34.683 --> 00:00:37.974
Your turn. Say: Fidelity.

10
00:00:40.833 --> 00:00:41.858
Fidelity.

11
00:00:42.617 --> 00:00:43.838
Equivalence.

12
00:00:44.850 --> 00:00:49.415
Equivalence means the effect of producing the same meaning or impact in another language.

13
00:00:50.017 --> 00:00:53.247
Translators look for equivalence of effect, not of words.

14
00:00:53.850 --> 00:00:56.944
Your turn. Say: Equivalence.

15
00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:01.045
Equivalence.

16
00:01:01.783 --> 00:01:03.075
Untranslatable.

17
00:01:04.083 --> 00:01:08.126
Untranslatable means impossible to express fully in another language.

18
00:01:08.717 --> 00:01:11.645
The Khmer word is often called untranslatable.

19
00:01:12.250 --> 00:01:15.216
Your turn. Say: Untranslatable.

20
00:01:18.067 --> 00:01:19.555
Untranslatable.

21
00:01:20.317 --> 00:01:21.550
Domestication.

22
00:01:22.550 --> 00:01:26.802
Domestication means adapting a text so it feels familiar to target readers.

23
00:01:27.417 --> 00:01:30.775
Domestication replaced local dishes with familiar ones.

24
00:01:31.383 --> 00:01:34.210
Your turn. Say: Domestication.

25
00:01:37.067 --> 00:01:38.346
Domestication.

26
00:01:39.083 --> 00:01:40.108
Foreignization.

27
00:01:41.117 --> 00:01:44.753
Foreignization means keeping the foreign features of the original visible.

28
00:01:45.350 --> 00:01:48.441
Foreignization preserves the original names and customs.

29
00:01:49.050 --> 00:01:52.352
Your turn. Say: Foreignization.

30
00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:56.271
Foreignization.

31
00:01:57.017 --> 00:01:57.902
Nuance.

32
00:01:58.917 --> 00:02:01.799
Nuance means a subtle difference in meaning or tone.

33
00:02:02.383 --> 00:02:05.103
Much of the poem's nuance was lost in translation.

34
00:02:05.717 --> 00:02:08.241
Your turn. Say: Nuance.

35
00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:12.159
Nuance.

36
00:02:12.917 --> 00:02:13.744
Register.

37
00:02:14.750 --> 00:02:17.713
Register means the level of formality of language.

38
00:02:18.317 --> 00:02:21.268
The translator kept the formal register of the letter.

39
00:02:21.883 --> 00:02:24.698
Your turn. Say: Register.

40
00:02:27.533 --> 00:02:28.314
Register.

41
00:02:29.050 --> 00:02:30.307
Interpretation.

42
00:02:31.317 --> 00:02:34.907
Interpretation means the act of explaining or rendering meaning.

43
00:02:35.517 --> 00:02:38.375
Every translation is also an interpretation.

44
00:02:38.983 --> 00:02:41.520
Your turn. Say: Interpretation.

45
00:02:44.367 --> 00:02:45.716
Interpretation.

46
00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:50.081
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:50.917 --> 00:02:55.972
Visal, you translated a Khmer folk tale into English. What was the hardest part?

48
00:02:57.083 --> 00:03:05.796
The idioms. Some of them cannot be translated directly, so they had to be replaced by an English expression with a similar effect.

49
00:03:06.883 --> 00:03:09.162
Isn't that a betrayal of the original?

50
00:03:10.250 --> 00:03:19.404
Not necessarily. If fidelity means preserving the impact on the reader, then a word-for-word version could be considered less faithful.

51
00:03:20.517 --> 00:03:22.841
And the forms of address for older people?

52
00:03:23.950 --> 00:03:34.068
Those I kept, deliberately. Readers should be reminded that the story comes from another culture, so some foreignness must be preserved.

53
00:03:35.233 --> 00:03:37.082
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:37.917 --> 00:03:41.762
Grammar focus: Passive with modal (can be, must be).

55
00:03:42.062 --> 00:03:43.203
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:43.917 --> 00:03:46.961
Some idioms cannot be translated directly.

57
00:03:47.850 --> 00:03:50.929
A word-for-word version could be considered less faithful.

58
00:03:51.817 --> 00:03:54.373
Some foreignness must be preserved for the reader.

59
00:03:55.333 --> 00:04:06.473
Choose the modal for the exact meaning: can/cannot be (possibility), must be (necessity), should be (recommendation), could be (tentative suggestion). The passive keeps the focus on the text, not the translator.

60
00:04:07.217 --> 00:04:11.274
Be careful! Don't say: Some idioms cannot translated directly.

61
00:04:13.233 --> 00:04:16.719
Say: Some idioms cannot be translated directly.

62
00:04:17.019 --> 00:04:18.844
Modal passive needs 'be'.

63
00:04:19.633 --> 00:04:22.588
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:24.749
Faithful to What?.

65
00:04:25.049 --> 00:04:31.101
Translation has always involved a tension between fidelity to the source and readability in the target language.

66
00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:37.873
A literal rendering may preserve the words, yet the nuance and register of the original can be lost entirely.

67
00:04:38.867 --> 00:04:44.581
Domestication makes a text feel natural to its new readers, although it risks erasing cultural difference.

68
00:04:45.567 --> 00:04:50.654
Foreignization, by contrast, insists that difference should be noticed rather than smoothed away.

69
00:04:51.667 --> 00:04:57.579
Most translators, in practice, must be prepared to move between both strategies within a single page.

70
00:04:58.667 --> 00:05:02.959
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:03.817 --> 00:05:06.130
Some jokes simply blank translated.

72
00:05:06.430 --> 00:05:10.269
A, cannot be. B, cannot. C, can't been.

73
00:05:13.833 --> 00:05:15.868
The answer is A: cannot be.

74
00:05:17.017 --> 00:05:20.282
Keeping original names and customs visible is called blank.

75
00:05:20.582 --> 00:05:24.967
A, domestication. B, foreignization. C, equivalence.

76
00:05:28.500 --> 00:05:30.941
The answer is B: foreignization.

77
00:05:32.083 --> 00:05:34.385
A subtle difference in meaning is a blank.

78
00:05:34.685 --> 00:05:38.419
A, register. B, nuance. C, fidelity.

79
00:05:41.967 --> 00:05:43.653
The answer is B: nuance.

80
00:05:44.817 --> 00:05:46.828
Choose the most tentative statement.

81
00:05:47.128 --> 00:05:54.520
A, This version must be rejected.. B, This version could be seen as less faithful.. C, This version is wrong..

82
00:05:58.067 --> 00:06:01.912
The answer is B: This version could be seen as less faithful..

83
00:06:03.050 --> 00:06:06.814
According to the reading, what may a literal translation lose?

84
00:06:07.114 --> 00:06:12.498
A, nuance and register. B, the words. C, its readers' interest in grammar.

85
00:06:16.033 --> 00:06:18.729
The answer is A: nuance and register.

86
00:06:19.933 --> 00:06:23.282
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:24.117 --> 00:06:27.684
What might be lost when a phrase is described as 'untranslatable'?

88
00:06:44.483 --> 00:06:46.007
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:46.307 --> 00:07:19.420
When a phrase is called untranslatable, what is usually lost is not its basic meaning but its emotional and cultural nuance. A translator can explain the idea in several words, yet the humour or warmth of the original may disappear. For example, Khmer terms of respect for elders cannot be rendered fully by 'sir' or 'madam'. Some argue that a good footnote solves the problem. I would say it helps, but reading a footnote is never the same as feeling the word.

90
00:07:19.720 --> 00:07:21.278
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:22.183 --> 00:07:27.262
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and passive with modal (can be, must be).

92
00:07:27.562 --> 00:07:32.920
Today's value is cultural sensitivity. Handle every culture's words with care and respect.

93
00:07:33.220 --> 00:07:35.756
See you in Unit ten. Goodbye!

94
00:07:36.056 --> 00:07:38.557
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

